<Project>

  <!-- ====================================================================================
       PART 1 — Run the AspectInjector weave OUT OF PROCESS.

       WHY. AspectInjector 2.9.0 registers an IN-PROCESS MSBuild task (AspectInjectorTask,
       AfterTargets="CoreCompile") that rewrites obj\...\<App>.dll with Mono.Cecil — and leaks
       file handles into the MSBuild node that hosts it:

         - FluentIL.PatcherBase.Process opens the target assembly with
           FileAccess.ReadWrite + FileShare.Read (Cecil ReaderParameters.ReadWrite=true) but
           only disposes it inside WriteAssembly. When the assembly contains NO aspects or
           injections — e.g. a consumer that merely references this package and declares no
           [Autosave] class yet — PatchAssembly returns false, WriteAssembly never runs, and
           the WRITE-capable handle on obj\...\<App>.dll leaks until a GC the build cannot
           force (an in-build GC.Collect + WaitForPendingFinalizers was tried and does not
           reclaim it in time).
         - The KnownReferencesAssemblyResolver it creates caches an open AssemblyDefinition
           (FileShare.Read read handle) for every resolved reference and is never disposed,
           in any path. In a reused MSBuild worker node those handles block REBUILDS of the
           referenced projects for minutes.

       The visible symptom, deterministic on small executable consumers (console, WinUI):

           error MSB4018: The "CreateAppHost" task failed unexpectedly.
           System.IO.IOException: The process cannot access the file '...\<App>.dll'
           because it is being used by another process.

       because the SDK's _CreateAppHost runs right after the weave and reads the Win32
       resources out of that same dll. Heavy builds (MAUI) dodge it only by timing. See
       docs/aspectinjector-2.9.0-apphost-lock.md in the repository for the full forensics.

       THE FIX. Suppress the in-process weave (AspectInjector_Enabled=false) and run the very
       same AspectInjectorTask, with the very same parameters, in a SHORT-LIVED CHILD MSBUILD
       PROCESS (Nucs.JsonSettings.Autosave.Weave.proj next to this file). The OS closes every
       handle at process exit, so by the time this target returns the woven assembly is
       provably unlocked, whatever AspectInjector forgot to dispose. The child is plain
       MSBuild + the task dll already restored in the AspectInjector package — no extra
       binaries are shipped and no weaving semantics change (the task weaves the whole
       assembly, so aspects from ANY package, not just ours, are processed exactly as
       before).

       KNOBS.
         <AspectInjector_Enabled>false</AspectInjector_Enabled>
             still disables weaving outright, exactly as documented by AspectInjector.
             (Only safe if the project declares no [Autosave]/[NotifyChanges] classes —
             they would compile and silently never work.)
         <NucsJsonSettingsOutOfProcWeave>false</NucsJsonSettingsOutOfProcWeave>
             opts back into AspectInjector's stock in-process weave (the pre-2.3.0
             behaviour, subject to the lock bug above). Shared by this package and
             Nucs.JsonSettings.NotifyChanges so one property governs the one weave.
         <AspectInjector_Location>...</AspectInjector_Location>
             honoured here like AspectInjector's own targets honour it: it must point at
             the AspectInjector.dll task assembly to use.

       TWO PACKAGES, ONE WEAVE. Nucs.JsonSettings.NotifyChanges ships a mirror of this file
       (NucsNotifyChanges_* names, see the note there). Whichever of the two is imported
       FIRST sees AspectInjector_Enabled not-yet-false, claims mode 'outofproc' and flips
       the property; the second then reads 'false', concludes 'off' and stays dormant —
       deliberately, so the weave (and the re-sign below) runs once, not twice. Should both
       weave targets ever run anyway (a future refactor breaking that chain), the
       .aspectsinjected stamp comparison in Inputs/Outputs still makes the second a no-op,
       because weaving twice would double every aspect's advice — a setter that saves twice
       per assignment.
       ==================================================================================== -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <NucsJsonSettingsOutOfProcWeave Condition="'$(NucsJsonSettingsOutOfProcWeave)' == ''">true</NucsJsonSettingsOutOfProcWeave>

    <!-- Duplicated from AspectInjector.targets on purpose: import order differs between a
         NuGet consumer (their file first) and this repository's Directory.Build.targets
         (this file first), so these may or may not be defaulted yet. The guards make the
         duplication idempotent. -->
    <AspectInjector_Verbose Condition="'$(AspectInjector_Verbose)' == ''">false</AspectInjector_Verbose>
    <AspectInjector_Debug Condition="'$(AspectInjector_Debug)' == ''">false</AspectInjector_Debug>

    <!-- off:       consumer (or the sibling package, see above) already disabled the
                    in-process weave; nothing to do, and re-signing is moot.
         inproc:    consumer opted out of the out-of-process fix; AspectInjector's stock
                    target runs untouched.
         outofproc: default; suppress the stock target and weave via the child process. -->
    <_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode Condition="'$(AspectInjector_Enabled)' == 'false'">off</_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode>
    <_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode)' == '' and '$(NucsJsonSettingsOutOfProcWeave)' == 'false'">inproc</_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode>
    <_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode)' == ''">outofproc</_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode>

    <!-- Works in both import orders: if AspectInjector.targets already ran, this overwrites
         its 'true'; if it runs after us, its own default is conditioned on empty and keeps
         our 'false'. -->
    <AspectInjector_Enabled Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode)' == 'outofproc'">false</AspectInjector_Enabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Mirrors AspectInjector_InjectAspectsCore: same anchors (AfterTargets/BeforeTargets on
       CoreCompile/AfterCompile), same incrementality (the .aspectsinjected stamp equal to the
       assembly's own timestamp), so mode switches never confuse the up-to-date check. The two
       extra BeforeTargets pin this weave BEFORE either package's re-sign target — the re-signs
       are anchored to the (suppressed, but still scheduled) AspectInjector_InjectAspects and
       would otherwise be free to run first, restoring a signature the weave then invalidates
       again AND touching the stamp in a way that marks this target falsely up to date. MSBuild
       ignores Before/AfterTargets naming targets a project never defines, so naming the
       NotifyChanges re-sign here is safe when only this package is referenced.

       Condition notes:
         - _InjectAspectsDependsOn is set unconditionally by AspectInjector.targets, so it is
           the execution-time marker for "AspectInjector is actually in this project". This
           repository imports the present file into EVERY project via Directory.Build.targets,
           including ones with no weaver; without the marker they would spawn a pointless
           child process per build.
         - DesignTimeBuild: IDE design-time builds must stay process-cheap, and nothing
           consumes the woven IL there. -->
  <Target Name="NucsAutosave_WeaveOutOfProc"
          AfterTargets="CoreCompile"
          BeforeTargets="_TimeStampAfterCompile;AfterCompile;NucsAutosave_ResignAfterWeaving;NucsNotifyChanges_ResignAfterWeaving"
          Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode)' == 'outofproc' and '$(_InjectAspectsDependsOn)' != '' and '$(DesignTimeBuild)' != 'true' and '@(IntermediateAssembly)' != ''"
          Inputs="@(IntermediateAssembly->'%(FullPath)')"
          Outputs="%(IntermediateAssembly.FullPath).aspectsinjected">

    <PropertyGroup>
      <_NucsAutosaveWeaveAssembly>@(IntermediateAssembly->'%(FullPath)')</_NucsAutosaveWeaveAssembly>
      <_NucsAutosaveWeaveRefsFile>$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveAssembly).aspectrefs</_NucsAutosaveWeaveRefsFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- MSBuild's command line splits -property: values on ';' with no escape a targets file
         can reliably produce, so a semicolon anywhere in the intermediate path reaches the
         child as a torn property and dies as a cryptic MSB1006. Fail it here, by name.
         (The stock in-process weave would build such a project - and produce an executable
         that cannot START, because ';' is also the separator of the host's trusted-platform-
         assemblies list: "Failed to create CoreCLR, HRESULT: 0x80070057". The path is broken
         for .NET either way; this error is just the honest version.) '%3B' is MSBuild's
         escape for ';', unescaped when the condition is evaluated - written literally, a raw
         semicolon inside a condition is a list separator. -->
    <Error Condition="$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveAssembly.Contains('%3B'))"
           Code="NJS1007"
           Text="Nucs.JsonSettings.Autosave: the intermediate assembly path '$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveAssembly)' contains a semicolon, which cannot be passed to the out-of-process weave (MSBuild splits command-line properties on ';') - and which .NET executables cannot run from at all (';' separates the runtime's assembly list). Rename the directory. For a library build that must stay put, &lt;NucsJsonSettingsOutOfProcWeave&gt;false&lt;/NucsJsonSettingsOutOfProcWeave&gt; restores the in-process weave, subject to the file-lock bug it carries." />

    <!-- Content-awareness: skip the weave when the assembly ALREADY carries AspectInjector's
         injected IL. The stamp discipline above is timestamp-only and other tooling is free
         to move the assembly past the stamp WITHOUT recompiling it - MAUI's XamlC (guarded
         below), and equally Fody, ILRepack, obfuscators, any post-compile rewriter. Re-weaving
         a woven assembly stacks the advice (measured: one save per write becomes two, then
         three, one per incremental build), so the stamp can never be trusted alone. The
         '__a$_instance' identifier is the aspect-singleton member AspectInjector plants in
         every assembly it processes; a fresh CoreCompile output never contains it (source
         code cannot reference it), so 'marker present' is precisely 'this file was woven and
         not recompiled since'. Read as text: the identifier is ASCII, and ASCII runs survive
         lossy UTF-8 decoding of arbitrary binary intact. The read costs one pass over a file
         this target was about to rewrite anyway, and only ever runs when the stamp says the
         assembly is newer than the last weave. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_NucsAutosaveAlreadyWoven>false</_NucsAutosaveAlreadyWoven>
      <_NucsAutosaveAlreadyWoven Condition="Exists('$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveAssembly)') and $([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveAssembly)').Contains('__a$_instance'))">true</_NucsAutosaveAlreadyWoven>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <Message Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveAlreadyWoven)' == 'true'"
             Importance="low"
             Text="Nucs.JsonSettings.Autosave: $(MSBuildProjectName) is already woven (a post-compile step moved it past the weave stamp without recompiling); skipping re-weave, re-stamping." />

    <!-- Locate the AspectInjector task assembly, first existing candidate wins:
           1. the consumer's explicit AspectInjector_Location (AspectInjector's own knob);
           2. derived from the AspectInjector.Analyzer item the package injects into
              @(Analyzer) — version-proof, because it follows whatever version restore
              actually resolved: <pkg>/analyzers/dotnet/cs/ -> <pkg>/binaries/;
           3. the NuGet cache path of the exact version this package depends on, for the
              rare consumer that strips analyzers with ExcludeAssets. -->
    <ItemGroup>
      <_NucsAutosaveInjectorProbe Include="$(AspectInjector_Location)" Condition="'$(AspectInjector_Location)' != ''" />
      <_NucsAutosaveInjectorProbe Include="@(Analyzer->'%(RootDir)%(Directory)../../../binaries/AspectInjector.dll')" Condition="'%(Analyzer.Filename)' == 'AspectInjector.Analyzer'" />
      <_NucsAutosaveInjectorProbe Include="$(NuGetPackageRoot)aspectinjector/2.9.0/binaries/AspectInjector.dll" Condition="'$(NuGetPackageRoot)' != ''" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_NucsAutosaveInjectorDll Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveInjectorDll)' == '' and Exists('%(_NucsAutosaveInjectorProbe.FullPath)')">%(_NucsAutosaveInjectorProbe.FullPath)</_NucsAutosaveInjectorDll>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- A hard error, not a fallback to the in-process weave: silently weaving in-process
         would re-arm the CreateAppHost lock this target exists to fix, and silently NOT
         weaving would ship [Autosave] setters that never save. Waived when the weave is
         being skipped as already-woven - nothing needs the task assembly then, and a
         consumer who stripped the analyzers should not fail a build that has no work. -->
    <Error Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveInjectorDll)' == '' and '$(_NucsAutosaveAlreadyWoven)' != 'true'"
           Code="NJS1005"
           Text="Nucs.JsonSettings.Autosave: cannot locate AspectInjector.dll for the out-of-process weave (probed AspectInjector_Location, the AspectInjector analyzer path, and the NuGet cache). Set &lt;AspectInjector_Location&gt; to the task assembly, or set &lt;NucsJsonSettingsOutOfProcWeave&gt;false&lt;/NucsJsonSettingsOutOfProcWeave&gt; to use AspectInjector's stock in-process weave." />

    <PropertyGroup>
      <!-- An empty value would trip MSB1005 on the child command line; false matches the
           task's own default. -->
      <_NucsAutosaveWeaveOptimize>$(Optimize)</_NucsAutosaveWeaveOptimize>
      <_NucsAutosaveWeaveOptimize Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveOptimize)' == ''">false</_NucsAutosaveWeaveOptimize>

      <!-- Self-referential host selection — no PATH assumptions: a .NET (Core) MSBuild
           re-runs its own MSBuild.dll through the dotnet host, a Framework MSBuild (VS)
           re-runs its own MSBuild.exe. -->
      <_NucsAutosaveWeaveMSBuild Condition="'$(MSBuildRuntimeType)' == 'Core' and '$(DOTNET_HOST_PATH)' != ''">&quot;$(DOTNET_HOST_PATH)&quot; exec &quot;$([System.IO.Path]::Combine('$(MSBuildBinPath)','MSBuild.dll'))&quot;</_NucsAutosaveWeaveMSBuild>
      <_NucsAutosaveWeaveMSBuild Condition="'$(MSBuildRuntimeType)' == 'Core' and '$(DOTNET_HOST_PATH)' == ''">dotnet exec &quot;$([System.IO.Path]::Combine('$(MSBuildBinPath)','MSBuild.dll'))&quot;</_NucsAutosaveWeaveMSBuild>
      <_NucsAutosaveWeaveMSBuild Condition="'$(MSBuildRuntimeType)' != 'Core'">&quot;$([System.IO.Path]::Combine('$(MSBuildBinPath)','MSBuild.exe'))&quot;</_NucsAutosaveWeaveMSBuild>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- The reference list goes through a file, not the command line: a large project's
         references overflow the ~32K Windows command-line limit many times over. -->
    <WriteLinesToFile Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveAlreadyWoven)' != 'true'"
                      File="$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveRefsFile)" Lines="@(ReferencePath->'%(FullPath)')" Overwrite="true" />

    <!-- -nodeReuse:false so the child leaves no persistent worker behind — a reused node
         holding leaked handles is the second face of this very bug. Weaver diagnostics come
         back in canonical MSBuild error/warning format on stdout, which Exec re-logs as real
         errors/warnings in this build; a failing weave fails the Exec (child MSBuild exits
         non-zero) and therefore the build, same as the in-process task. -->
    <Exec Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveAlreadyWoven)' != 'true'"
          Command="$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveMSBuild) &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Nucs.JsonSettings.Autosave.Weave.proj&quot; -nologo -nodeReuse:false -verbosity:quiet &quot;-consoleLoggerParameters:ErrorsOnly;NoSummary&quot; &quot;-property:NucsWeaveAssembly=$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveAssembly)&quot; &quot;-property:NucsWeaveReferencesFile=$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveRefsFile)&quot; &quot;-property:NucsWeaveInjector=$(_NucsAutosaveInjectorDll)&quot; -property:NucsWeaveOptimize=$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveOptimize) -property:NucsWeaveVerbose=$(AspectInjector_Verbose) -property:NucsWeaveAttachDebugger=$(AspectInjector_Debug)" />

    <!-- Same stamp discipline as AspectInjector's own target: marker time == assembly time
         means up to date; a recompile moves the assembly past the marker and re-weaves. Runs
         on the skip path too - that is the point of the skip: the assembly is woven, so the
         stamp may honestly say so, and the next build is up to date again. -->
    <Touch Files="%(IntermediateAssembly.FullPath).aspectsinjected" AlwaysCreate="true" Time="%(IntermediateAssembly.ModifiedTime)" />
  </Target>

  <!-- Guard against OTHER post-compile IL rewriters breaking the stamp discipline.

       .NET MAUI's XamlC target (Microsoft.Maui.Controls.targets) is AfterTargets="AfterCompile"
       and rewrites the intermediate assembly with Cecil AFTER the weave above ran, moving the
       assembly's timestamp past the .aspectsinjected marker. On the NEXT incremental build
       CoreCompile is up to date and skipped, but the weave target sees assembly-newer-than-stamp
       and re-weaves the ALREADY WOVEN assembly — and AspectInjector is not idempotent: measured
       on a probe app, each re-weave stacks another copy of the advice, so one setter write saved
       once after a clean build, twice after the next incremental build, three times after the one
       after that. Re-stamping to "now" right after XamlC keeps the marker at least as new as the
       rewritten assembly, exactly like the re-sign target below does for sn -R; a real source
       change still moves the assembly past the marker through CoreCompile and re-weaves once, as
       it should. Avalonia needs no such guard — its CompileAvaloniaXaml runs INSIDE CoreCompile
       (TargetsTriggeredByCompilation), so the weave always runs after it and the stamp stays
       authoritative. MSBuild ignores AfterTargets naming a target the project never defines, so
       this is inert outside MAUI.

       Rewriters this target does NOT know by name (Fody, ILRepack, obfuscators...) are caught by
       the content check inside the weave target instead: a re-armed stamp then costs one
       marker-detection read per build, not a stacked weave. This XamlC restamp stays because it
       is cheaper still — with it, the incremental build stays fully up to date and the weave
       target does not run at all. -->
  <Target Name="NucsAutosave_RestampAfterXamlC"
          AfterTargets="XamlC"
          Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode)' != 'off' and '$(_InjectAspectsDependsOn)' != '' and '$(DesignTimeBuild)' != 'true' and '@(IntermediateAssembly)' != ''">
    <Touch Files="@(IntermediateAssembly->'%(FullPath).aspectsinjected')" AlwaysCreate="true" />
  </Target>

  <!-- ====================================================================================
       PART 2 — Restore the strong-name signature that IL weaving invalidates.

       WHY THIS EXISTS. AspectInjector rewrites the compiled assembly after CoreCompile.
       The compiler has already signed that assembly by then, so any post-compile edit leaves
       the signature describing bytes that no longer exist. AspectInjector used to re-sign the
       result itself; 2.9.0 removed the feature outright:

           "Breaking change: resigning assemblies feature is retired as it no longer
            supported by MS"
           see https://github.com/pamidur/aspect-injector/releases/tag/2.9.0

       Measured on a clean A/B of a two-project probe (net8.0, full SignAssembly):

           AspectInjector_Enabled=false   sn -vf App.dll -> "is valid"
           AspectInjector_Enabled=true    sn -vf App.dll -> "Strong name validation failed."

       Both the aspect-declaring assembly and every consuming assembly are affected. The
       aspect-declaring one cannot opt out of weaving to dodge this, because AspectInjector
       injects the aspect's global singleton field during that pass; building it with
       AspectInjector_Enabled=false fails the consumer with
       "Aspect doesn't have global singleton injected".

       Downgrading is not an escape either. 2.8.2 still re-signs, but it does so via the
       GetFrameworkSdkPath task, which hard-fails under `dotnet build`:
       "error MSB4803: The task GetFrameworkSdkPath is not supported on the .NET Core version
       of MSBuild" — which is precisely why the feature was retired.

       HOW BAD IS AN INVALID SIGNATURE. Usually inert, never correct. .NET 5+ does not verify
       strong-name signatures at load time at all, and .NET Framework has bypassed
       verification for full-trust assemblies since 3.5 SP1. It still matters for `sn -vf` in
       a compliance gate, for the GAC, and for anything that verifies deliberately — and this
       repository strong-names on purpose (see Directory.Build.props), so shipping a package
       that silently invalidates a consumer's signature is not acceptable.

       WHAT THIS DOES. After the weave, if the project is really signing (not delay-signing,
       not public-signing) it re-signs the intermediate assembly with the same key the
       compiler used. If sn.exe cannot be found it warns rather than failing the build: the
       consumer's build is not the place to hard-stop over a signature that the runtime is
       not going to check.

       Opt out with <NucsAutosaveResignAfterWeaving>false</NucsAutosaveResignAfterWeaving>.
       ==================================================================================== -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <NucsAutosaveResignAfterWeaving Condition="'$(NucsAutosaveResignAfterWeaving)' == ''">true</NucsAutosaveResignAfterWeaving>

    <!-- Delay-signed and public-signed assemblies never carried a valid signature to begin
         with, so there is nothing to restore and re-signing them would be wrong.

         The last clause reads the weave MODE, not AspectInjector_Enabled: in the default
         out-of-process mode Part 1 sets AspectInjector_Enabled=false as a suppression
         mechanism while the assembly absolutely still gets woven — keying off the raw
         property here would silently skip the re-sign exactly when it is needed. 'off'
         (weaving truly disabled, whether by the consumer or because the sibling
         NotifyChanges package claimed the weave first) is the one state with nothing to
         re-sign — and when the sibling claimed it, the sibling's own re-sign target covers
         the assembly. -->
    <_NucsAutosaveShouldResign>false</_NucsAutosaveShouldResign>
    <_NucsAutosaveShouldResign Condition="'$(NucsAutosaveResignAfterWeaving)' == 'true'
                                          and '$(SignAssembly)' == 'true'
                                          and '$(DelaySign)' != 'true'
                                          and '$(PublicSign)' != 'true'
                                          and '$(AssemblyOriginatorKeyFile)' != ''
                                          and '$(_NucsAutosaveWeaveMode)' != 'off'">true</_NucsAutosaveShouldResign>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- sn.exe ships with the .NET Framework SDK and is Windows-only. The glob is evaluated
       once per project evaluation and is narrow enough not to be a measurable cost. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT' and '$(_NucsAutosaveShouldResign)' == 'true'">
    <_NucsSnCandidate Include="$([System.Environment]::GetFolderPath(SpecialFolder.ProgramFilesX86))\Microsoft SDKs\Windows\v*\bin\NETFX * Tools\x64\sn.exe" />
    <_NucsSnCandidate Include="$([System.Environment]::GetFolderPath(SpecialFolder.ProgramFilesX86))\Microsoft SDKs\Windows\v*\bin\NETFX * Tools\sn.exe" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Target batching: with Outputs referencing the item, this body runs once per candidate
       and the property is left holding the last one. MSBuild has no "take one item" idiom
       that reads better than this. -->
  <Target Name="NucsAutosave_LocateSn" Outputs="%(_NucsSnCandidate.Identity)">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_NucsSnExe>%(_NucsSnCandidate.FullPath)</_NucsSnExe>
    </PropertyGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- Still anchored to AspectInjector_InjectAspects: MSBuild fires AfterTargets even for a
       target whose Condition was false, so the suppressed stock target remains a valid
       trigger in out-of-process mode — and the BeforeTargets on the weave target above
       guarantees the weave has actually happened by the time this runs. -->
  <Target Name="NucsAutosave_ResignAfterWeaving"
          AfterTargets="AspectInjector_InjectAspects"
          DependsOnTargets="NucsAutosave_LocateSn"
          Condition="'$(_NucsAutosaveShouldResign)' == 'true'">

    <Exec Condition="'$(_NucsSnExe)' != ''"
          Command="&quot;$(_NucsSnExe)&quot; -q -R &quot;@(IntermediateAssembly->'%(FullPath)')&quot; &quot;$(AssemblyOriginatorKeyFile)&quot;"
          StandardOutputImportance="low"
          ContinueOnError="true">
      <Output TaskParameter="ExitCode" PropertyName="_NucsSnExitCode" />
    </Exec>

    <!-- CRITICAL, and the reason this is not just an Exec.

         The weave target is incremental on
             Inputs  = the intermediate assembly
             Outputs = <intermediate assembly>.aspectsinjected
         and it stamps that marker with the assembly's modified time, so the two come out
         equal and the next build correctly skips the weave.

         sn -R rewrites the assembly in place, which moves its timestamp PAST the marker.
         Without the re-stamp below, the following build therefore decides the assembly is
         newer than the marker and weaves it a second time, on top of the advice already
         there. Build a third time and it is woven again. The symptom is not a build error
         but a settings file written N times per assignment, where N is the number of builds
         since the last clean, which is exactly the kind of defect that survives a green test
         run on a fresh clone and appears only on a developer's machine.

         Re-stamping to "now" (rather than to the assembly's time) keeps the marker at least
         as new as the assembly, which is what the up-to-date check wants. A source change
         still moves the assembly past the marker on the next build and weaving runs again,
         as it should. -->
    <Touch Condition="'$(_NucsSnExe)' != '' and '$(_NucsSnExitCode)' == '0'"
           Files="@(IntermediateAssembly->'%(FullPath).aspectsinjected')"
           AlwaysCreate="true" />

    <Warning Condition="'$(_NucsSnExe)' == ''"
             Code="NJS1001"
             Text="Nucs.JsonSettings.Autosave: this project is strong-named and its assembly was rewritten by AspectInjector, which invalidates the signature. sn.exe was not found, so the signature could not be restored. Install the .NET Framework SDK, or set &lt;NucsAutosaveResignAfterWeaving&gt;false&lt;/NucsAutosaveResignAfterWeaving&gt; to silence this. The assembly still loads and runs -- .NET 5+ does not verify strong names -- but it will fail an explicit `sn -vf` check." />

    <Warning Condition="'$(_NucsSnExe)' != '' and '$(_NucsSnExitCode)' != '0'"
             Code="NJS1002"
             Text="Nucs.JsonSettings.Autosave: sn.exe -R failed with exit code $(_NucsSnExitCode); the strong-name signature invalidated by IL weaving was not restored." />
  </Target>

</Project>
